{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 13, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli also known as Raymond of Antioch was a Crusader nobleman who played a significant role during the Crusades. Born around 1140 he was the eldest son of Raymond of Poitiers and Constance of Antioch the latter being the daughter and sole heiress of Bohemond II Prince of Antioch.\n\n### *highlighted section part 1*\nEarly Life\nRaymond's early years were spent in Antioch where his mother Constance held power as regent for his half-brother Bohemond III. His father Raymond of Poitiers was a prominent figure in the Crusader states known for his military skills and political acumen. Raymond III received a solid education fitting for his noble status and was likely trained in both the arts of governance and warfare. His upbringing under the influence of his mother and guardians in Antioch helped shape his future allegiances and ambitions.\n\nIn 1152 Raymond's mother Constance married Renaud de Châtillon a Frankish nobleman with a reputation for martial prowess and ambition. Renaud’s marriage to Constance brought him significant influence in Antioch and the surrounding Crusader territories. Raymond was raised alongside his half-brother Bohemond III and his stepfather Renault whose presence would have a lasting impact on his life and future endeavors.\n\n### Family and Inheritance\nRaymond inherited the County of Tripoli following the death of his uncle Raymond II in 1152. However as a minor he was placed under the guardianship of his mother Constance and stepfather Renaud. By the time he came of age around 1164 Raymond had gained control over his county. His inheritance included vast territories in modern-day Lebanon and Syria an area critical during the Crusades.\n\n### *highlighted section part 2*\nPolitical and Military Career\nRaymond’s political career was marked by his interactions with neighboring powers including the Muslim states of Syria and the Byzantine Empire. He was involved in several diplomatic and military conflicts notably against Nur ad-Din a powerful Zengid ruler who sought to expand his influence over Crusader territories. Raymond was also engaged in complex political maneuvering within the Crusader states often clashing with other prominent figures such as Baldwin IV of Jerusalem.\n\nOne of Raymond’s most notable early conflicts was with Joscelin III of Edessa who had been expelled from his county by Nur ad-Din and sought refuge in Tripoli. Raymond initially took Joscelin in but eventually turned against him likely due to pressure from Nur ad-Din. This shift in allegiance showcased Raymond’s pragmatic approach to politics where alliances were often temporary and shifted based on convenience and survival.\n\n### Marriage and Later Life\nIn 1165 Raymond married Eschiva of Bures a French noblewoman who was the widow of Hugh III of Jaffa. This marriage was likely a strategic move to strengthen ties with the Kingdom of Jerusalem. Eschiva had a daughter named Alice from her previous marriage who would later become a significant player in the Crusader states.\n\n### *highlighted section part 3*\nLater Years and Death\nRaymond III's later years were filled with ongoing conflicts and political intrigue. His relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem particularly with Baldwin IV became increasingly strained. Raymond was accused of conspiring with the Muslims against the Crusader states and was eventually captured by Baldwin in 1180. Imprisoned for three years he was released after swearing an oath of allegiance to the king.\n\nAfter his release Raymond continued to play an active role in the political landscape of the Crusader states. He participated in various military campaigns and diplomatic missions striving to protect his territories from the growing threats posed by Saladin’s forces. Raymond III died in 1187 during the Battle of Hattin a decisive conflict that marked the beginning of the end for the Crusader states in the Holy Land.\n\nHis legacy is remembered for his involvement in the complex political and military struggles of the era. Raymond’s life exemplifies the challenges faced by the Crusader nobles who had to navigate a treacherous environment filled with shifting alliances rivalries and external threats. Despite his flaws and controversies Raymond III’s actions had a lasting impact on the history of the Crusader states and the broader region.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.06853077560663223}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.00012003655137959868}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.006313735153526068}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.29494979977607727}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00038413776201196015}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -0.01811952330172062}, {"token": " also", "logprob": -1.730838656425476}, {"token": " known", "logprob": -0.04531657695770264}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.03303052857518196}, {"token": " Raymond", "logprob": -0.12591156363487244}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.4887281060218811}, {"token": " Antio", "logprob": -0.21861356496810913}, {"token": "ch", "logprob": -3.909988299710676e-05}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.2954932153224945}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.2654343843460083}, {"token": " Crus", "logprob": -1.5045514106750488}, {"token": "ader", "logprob": -0.00031537804170511663}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -1.2395145893096924}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.0543534941971302}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -1.2777925729751587}, {"token": " played", "logprob": -0.6655131578445435}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.04611284285783768}, {"token": " significant", "logprob": -0.3052492141723633}, {"token": " role", "logprob": -0.0190474484115839}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.6337767243385315}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.0038459161296486855}, {"token": " Crus", "logprob": -2.144155979156494}, {"token": "ades", "logprob": -0.7601308822631836}, {"token": 